a blanket of snow covers outfits but  does nothing to hide the horrors of this  former Nazi death camp  seventy years after it was liberated by  the Red Army around 300 former prisoners  gather in front of the camp's gate of  death they sit either side of the tracks  which carried many of them in cattle  trucks into this extermination factory  more than a million people were murdered  here  most of them Jews Halina bierenbaum  was just a child but remembers the hell  with tragic clarity all around us  electric barbed wire and rows of  barracks stinking mud and some figures  muddling through  I cannot tell whether they are old or  young women or men a disgusting mass of  people all in lousy wet rags with  numbers shaven heads those gray faces  with legs like sticks wearing those  muddy clogs nothing reminding you of  anything remotely human outfits was a  place of sophisticated torture of  efficient killing humanity stripped from  every inch of this huge site all these  years on the same message echoes around  the miserable walls we survivors do not  want how it best to be our children's  future a warning repeated for global  leaders gathered at the event there are  representatives today from 40 countries  you are good decent people and but  because of what we are and what this  place means your governments must stand  up to this new wave of hatred in London  a similar ceremony the government's  announced it's giving 50 million pounds  towards a new memorial and the Prince of  Wales added his thoughts about the  importance of remembering it enables us  to rally together to recognize the  workings of wickedness to exercise  vigilance and hopefully to stop such  atrocities in the future  the biting cold can't freeze the fiery  determination of these survivors the  worn seven decades on prejudice must  never become policy hatred must never be  tolerated  Jonathan Samuels Sky News
 

